The below post comes close.

> 1) Let you pet be your protector. Pets get experience for kills. And
> if you allow your let to get all the kills in the early game, they
> quickly become a formidable force in the game.

Play a healer to get a feel for your pet's level and HP: you start out
with a stethoscope, so this is easy to check.

> 2) Monster power and experience goes up with your characters
> experience. Ever wonder how you get newts on level one in the early
> game, but when you come back to level one at the end game you're
> getting fire giants and dragons? Well the types of monsters generated
> is based on your characters XL. So if you keep the XL low, then the
> danger/power of the monsters generated remains low too. Dungeon level
> factors in also.

Just say that it is based on the average of your level and dungeon
level.

> 1) Let your pet kill (and eat) everything. You stick close to your pet
> and pray when you are weak. Eat the lichen and whatever other veggies
> or food rations you may come across.

No need to let your pet eat everything. Eating does not increase its
level. If you can, try to beat him to the corpse: you'll usually lose
anyway.

> Enough money to pay the priest.

"in minetown", I would add.

> 3) AC is still critical. Wear any armor you come across as long as
> it's not cursed. You may even want to take a chance on a cursed item
> if the reward is great enough. For example I'd probably wear cursed
> banded mail early in the game.

I wouldn't, for two reasons:

(1) you will probably come across a dwarven or an elven mithril coat
before long, and you don't want to be stuck with that banded mail when
you do, and

(2) banded mail is *very* heavy, and will drastically worsen your
carrying capacity.

> 4) Get to the minetown priest, and get your naked AC to at least 1,
> which the priest will help you lower with each and every donation.

It may be worth to mention here that the amount you need to donate is
exactly 400 times your own level. So, if you manage to get there with
your experience level still at one, it'll cost 400 zorkmids per chat
session.

> Then you can go attack the rest of the dungeon and Mines.

And good luck from all of us.

